# Maintenance freeze for a Hostinger site during cutover.
#
# PREPEND this to the site's existing public_html/.htaccess, keeping everything
# already there below it. Remove it to lift the freeze.
#
# Purpose is to stop WRITES at the source, so nothing can change between the
# final resync and DNS propagation. A maintenance page only on Hetzner does not
# achieve this: Hostinger keeps serving until DNS moves, and during propagation
# some visitors still reach it and can still post.
#
# 503 + Retry-After is deliberate, and not cosmetic:
#   - 200 with "we're down" text tells search engines the real page now says
#     that. On a listings site, that is how you get deindexed.
#   - A 302 to a holding page has the same problem plus it caches badly.
#   - 503 with Retry-After is the documented "temporarily unavailable, come
#     back" signal. Crawlers retain the old page and retry.
#
# ALLOW_IP lets you keep testing the source while the public sees maintenance.
# Uncomment it and replace with your own address. Left out, the freeze is hard
# and applies to everyone including yourself, which is what every cutover so
# far has used.
#
# THE SENTINELS ARE NOT OPTIONAL. `resync-site.sh` strips the freeze from the
# DESTINATION copy by deleting between the two markers, because the sync copies
# .htaccess faithfully and a freeze that travels to Hetzner means the new site
# serves 503 to everyone the moment DNS moves -- indistinguishable from a
# failed migration. `audit-sites.sh` looks for the same marker. A block applied
# without them is stripped by neither. Copy this file whole; do not retype the
# rules and leave the markers off.
#
# The comment on the START line is free text -- date it, so a freeze left
# behind on some site months from now can be dated at a glance.

### MIGRATION-FREEZE-START -- added YYYY-MM-DD, remove this block to lift
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
	RewriteEngine On

	# Let the maintenance page itself through, or the rule loops.
	RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/maintenance\.html$

	# Your IP still sees the real site. Uncomment for a soft freeze.
	# RewriteCond %{REMOTE_ADDR} !^ALLOW_IP$

	# `-` means no substitution: set the status and let ErrorDocument below
	# serve the body. Rewriting to /maintenance.html instead would return the
	# page but discard the 503, which defeats the whole point.
	RewriteRule ^ - [R=503,L]
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
	# 900s is a hint, not a promise. Set it to roughly how long you expect the
	# cutover to take.
	Header always set Retry-After "900" "expr=%{REQUEST_STATUS} == 503"
</IfModule>

ErrorDocument 503 /maintenance.html
### MIGRATION-FREEZE-END
